  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes two kinds of crystal interfaces and how their atomic matching changes. In the top row, it shows grain boundaries within the same material. A high-angle grain boundary has two lattices meeting with a large misorientation, producing a strongly disturbed boundary region. A low-angle grain boundary has only a small mismatch, so the lattices remain mostly aligned except for a narrow line of defects. A twin boundary is a special, highly ordered case where one side is a mirror-like reorientation of the other, labeled as matrix and twin.\n\nThe bottom row compares interphase boundaries between different crystal structures or phases. An incoherent boundary shows poor atomic registry, with the two lattices fitting badly at the interface. A semi-coherent boundary shows partial matching: much of the interface aligns, but some mismatch remains and seems concentrated at periodic defect sites. A coherent boundary shows the best fit, where atoms line up continuously across the interface with minimal disruption.\n\nOverall, the figure illustrates a progression from disordered, highly mismatched interfaces to increasingly ordered and well-matched boundaries, emphasizing how crystallographic alignment controls boundary structure.",
